    "answer": "Here is my investigation report:\n\n## Findings\n\nThe test project sits at `tests/VisualRelay.Tests/` with **423 `.cs` files** (~430 xUnit test files). The xUnit runner config has `\"parallelizeTestCollections\": true` (line 3 of `xunit.runner.json`), meaning **each distinct test class runs in parallel with others** by default. However, many large test classes use the `public sealed partial class` pattern \u2014 splitting a single class across multiple files. Because xUnit treats each *class* (not each file) as one collection, all companion files of the same class execute **serially**, creating a parallelism bottleneck.\n\n### Existing split patterns already in use\n\nThe codebase already has an extensive companion-file convention (enforced by `SplitGuardVerificationTests.Conventions.cs`):\n\n| Partial class family | Files | Total [Fact]s | Lines |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `BackendConfigGeneratorTests` | 6 files (main + VisionTier, PerModelTimeout, Selectable, AliasConsistency, KimiK2_7Upstream) | 17 | 296 (main) |\n| `GitCommitterTests` | 4 files (main + CommitMsgHooks, RunBaseSquash, RunBaseSquashGuards) | 10+ | 267 (main) |\n| `GitCommitterAutoIncludeTests` | 5 files (main + Snapshot, Resilience, TasksDir, FirstInstance) | 14 | 284 (main) |\n| `RelayDriverResumeTests` | 7 files (main + CommitGate, ReAdd, ReAdd2, FlaggedWork, FlaggedWork2, FlaggedWork3) | 9+ | 162 (main) |\n| `RelayDriverGitCommitTests` | 4 files (main + SelfCommitSquash, ResumeCommit, GitignoredBackstop) | 10 | 299 (main) |\n| `SwivalSubagentRunnerWatchdogTests` | 5 files (main + CpuPulse, ActivityWatchdog, TierWindows, NonzeroExit) | 19 | ~281 (main) |\n| `SwivalSubagentRunnerTests` | 2 files (main + Timeout) | 10 | 281 (main) |\n| `Install5LauncherTests` | 2 files (main + CwdSandbox) | 20 (was) | 219 (main) |\n| `RelayDriverTests` | 3 files (main + BaselineVerify, Stage5Tests) | 13 | 293 (main) |\n| `MainWindowViewModelTests` | 8+ files (main + Status, ResumeButton, Reorder, Pause, ExceptionSurfacing, Bootstrap, RunnableGate) | ~? | 293 (main) |\n| `RelayDriverVerifyFixTests` | 5 files (main + Worktree, Escalation, Distill, ConvergenceGuard) | ~? | 300 (main) |\n| `WorktreeFilterTests` | 8 files (main + CopyRecords, EdgeCases, ResidualLeaks, ResidualDataLoss, DataLossFixes, RevertHardening, RevertHardening2) | ~? | 182 (main) |\n| `SwivalProfileSessionPinningTests` | 3 files (main + KimiMaxContext, EndToEnd) | ~? | 204 (main) |\n| `SwivalSubagentRunnerSandboxTests` | 2 files (main + SkipDirs) | ~? | 271 (main) |\n| `SwivalSubagentRunnerCommandFilterTests` | 2 files (main + Integration) | 15 | 197 (main) |\n\n**Total**: The baseline `[Fact]` count across the 12 \"oversized families\" tracked by `SplitGuardVerificationTests` is **189** (line 157 of `SplitGuardVerificationTests.cs`).\n\n### Non-partial classes at/near the 300-line cap\n\nThese are already standalone `sealed class` (not partial), so they already run as independent collections in parallel. However, they are all right at the 300-line limit and could be split further for better maintainability/responsiveness:\n\n- `RelayTaskRepositoryTests` (300 lines)\n- `RelayConfigLoaderTests` (300 lines)\n- `ObsidianBridgeSettingsTests` (300 lines)\n- `KeySetupPanelUiTests` (300 lines)\n- `AddAttachmentsTests` (300 lines)\n- `AddAttachmentsVisibilityTests` (300 lines)\n- `PackagingToolTests` (295 lines)\n- `MainWindowViewModelSettingsTests` (296 lines)\n- `TaskRewriteRunnerTests` (294 lines)\n- `MainWindowViewModelLayoutTests` (300 lines)\n- `CompletionTimeResolverTests` (297 lines)\n- `StageDetailViewModelTests` (299 lines)\n- `TargetedTestCommandTests` (299 lines)\n- `CommitTestRunners` (300 lines)\n- `SandboxPathInspectorTests` (298 lines)\n\n### Collection attributes (serialization constraints)\n\n- **`[CollectionDefinition(\"Headless\")]`** in `HeadlessCollectionDefinition.cs` \u2014 serializes all Avalonia headless UI tests on the single process-global dispatcher. Currently 50+ classes carry `[Collection(\"Headless\")]`.\n- **`[Collection(\"Watchdog\")]`** \u2014 serializes CPU-heavy subprocess watchdog tests (`SwivalSubagentRunnerWatchdogTests` family).\n- No other `[Collection]` or `[CollectionDefinition]` attributes exist.\n\n**Key constraint**: Companion files \"inherit\" `[Collection]` from the main class and must NOT redundantly declare it (enforced by `SplitGuardVerificationTests.Conventions.cs` line 35).\n\n### The parallelism bottleneck\n\n**Partial classes are NOT parallelized** \u2014 xUnit considers `WorktreeFilterTests` (split across 8 files) to be a single test class with all its `[Fact]` methods running serially. The partial-class pattern was introduced to stay under the 300-line `FileSizeGuard` limit (line 26 of `SplitGuardVerificationTests.cs` references a past count of \"12+\" violations before the split). But the structural split only addressed file-size, not parallelism.\n\n### Option assessment against codebase reality\n\n- **Option A** (`[Collection]` to split partial classes): Would not help \u2014 `[Collection]` groups classes into collections; it doesn't parallelize within a class. A partial class is one class.\n- **Option B** (separate top-level classes): The most impactful. Each companion file would become its own `public sealed class TestName` (e.g., `WorktreeFilterTests.CopyRecords` \u2192 class `WorktreeFilterCopyRecordsTests`), making each its own parallel xUnit collection.\n- **Option C** (convert `[Fact]` to `[Theory]`): Reduces serial `[Fact]` count within a collection but doesn't make the remaining facts run in parallel. Helpful for repetitive test patterns but limited impact on the total bottleneck.\n\nThe codebase already has a precedent for Option B \u2014 many test groups are already standalone `sealed class` (e.g., `RelayTaskRepositoryTests`, `TargetedTestCommandTests`, `PackagingToolTests`). The companion files using partial classes are the ones that need conversion.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"findings\": \"The test project (423 .cs files, xUnit v3, parallelizeTestCollections: true) has ~42 large partial-class families where multiple companion files share a single class declaration. Since xUnit treats each class as one collection, these partial-class aggregations run serially \u2014 the primary parallelism bottleneck. The existing split was file-size-driven (300-line guard), not parallelism-driven. The 12 'oversized families' tracked by SplitGuardVerificationTests aggregate 189 [Fact]s across ~47 companion files. Two special collections serialize certain tests: [Collection('Headless')] (Avalonia dispatcher, ~50 classes) and [Collection('Watchdog')] (CPU-burning subprocess tests). Non-partial classes at the 300-line cap (15+ files) already parallelize well. The most impactful approach is converting partial-class families into separate top-level classes so each companion file becomes its own xUnit collection.\",\n  \"constraints\": [\n    \"xUnit treats each class as one serial collection \u2014 partial-class companion files do NOT parallelize\",\n    \"FileSizeGuard enforces a strict 300-line maximum per .cs file (all src/tests/tools)\",\n    \"SplitGuardVerificationTests validates a baseline [Fact] count of 189 across 12 oversized families \u2014 any split must preserve the same total [Fact] count\",\n    \"Companion files must use 'public sealed partial class' and must NOT carry [Collection] (enforced by convention tests)\",\n    \"All test classes must be 'sealed' (sealed class or sealed partial class)\",\n    \"Avalonia headless UI tests (~50 classes) must remain in [Collection('Headless')] \u2014 serial on the shared dispatcher\",\n    \"SwivalSubagentRunnerWatchdogTests must remain in [Collection('Watchdog')] \u2014 serial to avoid CPU contention\",\n    \"Existing partial-class families share private helper methods and test infrastructure that would need extraction if split into separate classes\",\n    \"The TaskCompletionArchive, ActivityColumnTabsUi, RunAllModes, and other families already have companion files that could be promoted to standalone classes\",\n    \"Renaming classes would require updating any type references in tests or production code\"\n  ]\n}\n```",
